ef 17592186409918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409922 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129189" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409922 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409922 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-z$ that passes through the point $P(0,-2,4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409922 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef96fb6f-8651-5bcd-8700-e66e3e1a4bf5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409922 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2,4) & ={2}^{2}-{0}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =4-0-4 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-z & =0 \\\\ z & ={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(0,-2,4)$ is the hyperbolic paraboloid $z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409923 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186409927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409923 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a94aecad-6d06-5584-9ae6-ff5e4c972c6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409923 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186409922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409924 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186409925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409924 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bfbdcc24-f373-559b-9261-b97d80ffba78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409924 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186409920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409925 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186409926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409925 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d981e48e-0b28-5d2c-b93e-70be17e1dbbd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409925 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186409921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409926 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186409923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409926 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f363fade-7d87-58eb-bf21-09d29aa00d